#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078552 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9afe0d46-84e7-5937-9b42-170097a72493" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Side Length Using Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "17e8ec56-6fda-5294-b27c-9b3e312a5f75" :question/math-academy-id "e-3248" :question/problem "Given the triangle below, find the length of $\\overline{MN}$ rounded to one decimal place.\n\n![](images/db/db0c3be4d3f18a55bfbd763e4017bdabd6c27ba7b498e21909baa4aea3d44337.png)" :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{NO}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overline{MN}$ adjacent to the given angle $\\angle{N}$.\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (m∠N) &= \\frac{adjacent leg}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n\\cos (25^{∘}) &= \\frac{MN}{14} \\\\\nMN &= 14\\cos (25^{∘})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMN &= 14\\cos (25^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 14(0.90630…) \\\\\n&= 12.68830… \\\\\n&\\approx 12.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078257 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078551 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04354ab1-c6ec-534e-bb57-267f326c15fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Side Length Using Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "b4f8dd38-d62d-5940-b936-48f68e0693b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3249" :question/problem "Given the triangle below, find the length of $\\overline{NO}$ rounded to one decimal place.\n\n![](images/91/9108818debc297388dde73a7aa7689eac263c6ec033643c7e2bcdfb3677a8f44.png)" :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, notice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overline{MO}$ opposite the given angle $\\angle{N}$ and wish to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{NO}$.\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (m∠N) &= \\frac{opposite leg}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n\\sin (32^{∘}) &= \\frac{20}{NO} \\\\\nNO \\cdot \\sin (32^{∘}) &= 20 \\\\\nNO &= (20)/(\\sin (32^{∘}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nNO &= (20)/(\\sin (32^{∘})) \\\\\n&= (20)/((0.52991…)) \\\\\n&= 37.74159… \\\\\n&\\approx 37.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078258 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078553 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54289a8c-57cd-50b6-8b94-8628b2bc27be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Side Length Using Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "a74a938e-94a4-5a91-aced-4955dc8dfbf8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3250" :question/problem "Given the triangle below, find the length of $\\overline{BC}$ in terms of $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/8b/8b1f3d7e1d87c7021970f45279bb404b4e9998a9d351d1e95616dac98f80d45d.png)" :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}$\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overline{AB}$ adjacent to the given angle $\\angle A$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overline{BC}$ opposite $\\angle A$.\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (m∠A) &= \\frac{opposite leg}{adjacent leg} \\\\\n\\tan θ &= \\frac{BC}{15} \\\\\nBC &= 15\\tan θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078551 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04354ab1-c6ec-534e-bb57-267f326c15fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Side Length Using Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1b81862-5146-5f0a-8ac5-0fd79c0a2c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077469} {:answer/id #uuid "cf368945-778b-5d74-90d6-d2b82979f4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077470} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7a84db-08fb-51e4-b38d-a6f78cb89655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077471} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed5b9e8-a40d-55c4-8a76-c92577e695bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077472} {:answer/id #uuid "36c4bd36-3076-50a5-a9a1-65ac22047faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077473}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cd9bfa65-ff88-5011-a0c7-0a010f14b7ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077468}] :question/id #uuid "07fbac6a-971f-53dc-a7f0-99a5c49ba233" :question/math-academy-id "q-54238" :question/problem "![](images/32/32938bfdcb2ed9130304722b408b304802062b701bb35c68d7d3333c7cfe8ee6.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $AB^(―)$ rounded to one decimal place.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
